\section{Scheme}{

\code{aci_online()} composes the published \strong{discrete} Theorem 3 updates, which
retain the leading-order terms of the continuous-time conditional equations
under the explicit single-step discretization. Its finite-step output
approximates the continuous-time conditional distribution and is not
generally the exact posterior for an Euler-sampled record: for
\verb{dx = y dt + dW1}, \code{dy = dW2}, \code{y0 ~ N(0, 1)}, \code{dt = 0.1} and one observed
increment 0.2 it returns mean 0.2 and variance 0.9, where exact conditioning
on that Euler-sampled increment gives 0.1818 and 0.9091. The gap falls with
the step. \code{\link[=aci_smoother]{aci_smoother()}}
integrates the \strong{continuous} backward smoothing equations with an Euler
step of the same size. These are two discretizations of the same
continuous-time object and they agree only to first order in the step, so at
full lag \code{aci_online()} does \strong{not} reproduce \code{\link[=aci_smoother]{aci_smoother()}}. The gap
grows
with the length of the record; it is not a constant offset. On the packaged
ENSO partition (\code{l = 3}, \code{dt = 0.005}) the smoothed means differ by up to
1.89e-02 against a mean scale of 0.388 over 401 steps, and by up to 9.58e-02
against a scale of 2.22 over 4001 steps. The resulting ACI values differ by
up to 0.104 against a scale of 1.093 at 401 steps and 0.482 against 2.347 at
4001. \code{\link[=lag_table]{lag_table()}} and the causal influence range estimators use the
discrete scheme throughout, which is why \code{lt_diag()} and \code{aci()$aci} can
differ by that same amount. \code{meta$scheme} records which scheme produced a
path: \code{"theorem3_discrete"} here and on the lag table's reference smoother,
\code{"backward_ode_euler"} on \code{\link[=aci_smoother]{aci_smoother()}}.

The one exact boundary is the other end: at \code{lag = 0} the returned moments
are the filter moments, unchanged value for value.
}

\section{Range of the scheme}{

One diagnostic compares one step of hidden noise with the filter variance
at the start of each interval: the largest eigenvalue of \verb{Q Rf^-1 dt},
with \code{Rf} the filter covariance and \code{Q} the hidden noise covariance given
the observed increment. The update factor also depends on the hidden drift,
so this ratio is not a general stability or accuracy criterion. A ratio
above 1 warns of a step that can make the smoothed covariance exceed the
filter covariance. A ratio at or below 1 does not rule that out.
\code{aci_online()} and
\code{\link[=lag_table]{lag_table()}}, and \code{\link[=aci]{aci()}} and \code{\link[=aci_range]{aci_range()}} when they build a table, raise
one \code{aci_warn_leading_order} per call when a ratio is above 1. The
condition carries \code{ratio}, \code{index} and \code{time} for the interval it reports.
The returned values are those the scheme gives. A smaller \code{dt} lowers the
ratio, and at the first interval a wider prior does too; the message names
the prior only when it reports the first interval. Under
\code{regularize = "floor"} the intervals from the first floored filter
covariance on are left out: a floored value, and every filter covariance
propagated from it, says nothing about the model, and
\code{aci_warn_regularized} reports the floor. This holds for a filter formed
in the call and for a supplied \code{filter} that took a floor. A table already
built forms no update when it is passed to \code{\link[=aci]{aci()}} or \code{\link[=aci_range]{aci_range()}}, and
raises nothing more.
}

